## Run

Flutter **3.47.6** includes Dart **3.13.5**. Install the [official stable SDK](https://docs.flutter.dev/install),
add its `bin` directory to your login PATH, and run `flutter doctor -v`.
`.fvmrc` records the tested version for environments that already use FVM; FVM is optional.

```sh
# Only needed if setup was skipped or after checking out the project on another machine:
test -f .env || cp .env.example .env
make setup
make dev
```

Open http://127.0.0.1:{{ ports.WEB_PORT }}. Add a note, refresh, delete it, and try Settings → Appearance.
The web preview runs the same Dart application as the mobile targets. From fastDev, stop and restart
Dev after edits: its commands run without a terminal. For terminal hot reload use:

```sh
make devices
dart run tool/project.dart run -d chrome
# Or substitute an Android emulator / iOS simulator ID. Press r to hot reload.
```

`chrome` requires Google Chrome. Android requires Android Studio/SDK, a compatible JDK and accepted
SDK licenses. iOS requires full Xcode, its iOS SDK and a simulator/device; Apple Command Line Tools alone
are insufficient. Follow the [Android](https://docs.flutter.dev/platform-integration/android/setup)
and [iOS](https://docs.flutter.dev/platform-integration/ios/setup) setup guides.

## Commands

| Command | Result |
|---|---|
| `make setup` | Install exact locked packages and generate native/public configuration |
| `make dev` | Browser preview on the allocated port |
| `make check` | Formatting, strict analysis with zero warnings, unit and widget tests |
| `make test` | Unit/widget tests with configuration from `.env` |
| `make format` | Format Dart sources |
| `make build` | Release web output in `build/web/` |
| `make start` | Serve the existing release web output locally |
| `make devices` | List device IDs |
| `dart run tool/project.dart run -d <id>` | Native or Chrome development with hot reload |
| `make android` | Local release APK in `build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/` |
| `make ios` | Unsigned iOS release in `build/ios/iphoneos/` |
| `dart run tool/project.dart test --coverage` | Tests and `coverage/lcov.info` |

The generated Android release configuration uses the development signing key for local testing.
Configure your signing identity, application ID, icons, permissions and version before store distribution.
The iOS command produces an unsigned build; signing/archive/upload are separate steps.

## Design and styling

This project was created with **{% if choices.ui == 'glass' %}Liquid Glass{% else %}Material 3{% endif %}**
and **{% if choices.styling == 'tailwind' %}Mix / Tailwind utilities{% else %}Flutter widgets{% endif %}**.

The shared UI lives in `lib/ui/`: semantic colors in `tokens.dart`, panels in `surface.dart` and
`styled_panel.dart`, responsive navigation in `workspace_shell.dart`, background in `wallpaper.dart`.
`lib/app/theme.dart` defines typography, controls and both appearances. The desktop sidebar appears at
960 logical pixels and 560 pixels of available height; smaller windows use compact navigation.
Panels and notes scroll with the keyboard open.
{%- if choices.ui == 'glass' %} High contrast and accessible navigation disable blur and use opaque panels.
Collection items never create individual blur filters. The background is painted locally.{% endif %}
The app bundles no remote images. On the web, Flutter loads its CanvasKit renderer and default Roboto font
from Google's CDNs by default; `dart run tool/project.dart build --no-web-resources-cdn` serves both from `build/web`.

{% if choices.ui == 'glass' %}
Liquid Glass is a Flutter interpretation of macOS frosted surfaces: clipped backdrop blur, translucent
fills, bright edges and soft shadows. It works across the three targets; it is not a native SwiftUI
Liquid Glass material or a refraction shader. The selected design is recorded in `lib/ui/design.dart`.
{% else %}
Material 3 uses opaque themed surfaces, the responsive layout above and accessible Material controls.
The selected design is recorded in `lib/ui/design.dart`.
{% endif %}

{% if choices.styling == 'tailwind' %}
The styling adapter uses **Mix 2.2.1 + mix_winds 0.1.0-alpha.1**. Shared panels actually use
`p-6 rounded-3xl border border-line bg-panel`; product color aliases are provided through `TwConfig`.
This is a native Flutter implementation of a **subset** of Tailwind utilities, not Tailwind CSS, HTML,
or a WebView. The upstream package is experimental; keep the exact pins and test changes before
expanding its use. Unsupported classes fail explicitly through `onDiagnostic` rather than silently
changing the UI. Add aliases in the adapter and use `Theme.of(context)` for accessible controls.
Keep Flutter `Row`, `Column`, `Expanded`, `LayoutBuilder` and native form widgets for layout and input;
CSS flex and Flutter constraints differ. Read the upstream [adaptations](https://github.com/btwld/mix/blob/main/packages/mix_winds/FLUTTER_ADAPTATIONS.md)
and [API](https://pub.dev/packages/mix_winds) before adding utilities. No Node, CSS compiler or generator
is needed. Keep form semantics, hit targets, keyboard behavior and both themes in widget tests.
{% else %}
Shared panels use standard `Container`, `BoxDecoration`, `EdgeInsets` and Flutter theme tokens.
There is no styling dependency. Add visual primitives here instead of duplicating decorations across
feature screens.
{% endif %}

## Configuration and data

`.env` is the source of truth for the display name, application ID and preview port. `tool/config.dart`
reads it with dotenv without expanding variables, escapes platform formats, and forwards **only**
`APP_NAME` and `APP_SLUG` as compile-time JSON defines. `.env` is never bundled as an asset.
After changing configuration, restart Dev or rebuild. `APP_IDENTIFIER` is applied to Android and iOS;
`APP_SLUG` namespaces local storage. The internal Dart package is deliberately named `app`, so imports
stay stable when the product is renamed. Configuration updates modify the native metadata and web title.

`shared_preferences` stores appearance and the small **disposable demo notes**. It is not a transactional
or durable database. For important user data, implement the `NotesRepository` contract with a real store
(e.g. Drift/SQLite) or an API and add migrations, recovery and synchronization tests.
Web preferences belong to the browser origin; changing the preview port selects another origin.
There is no backend or authentication service configured yet.

## Code

`lib/app/` composes routing/theme; `lib/core/` holds configuration and shared adapters;
`lib/features/<feature>/` contains models, repositories, controllers and screens.
`test/` exercises validation, persistence, failures, concurrency, navigation and a phone with its keyboard open.
Native hosts are in `android/` and `ios/`; `web/` contains browser bootstrap files.

